1
00:00:00,792 --> 00:00:02,931
Hi, my name is Nishanth Vepachedu.

2
00:00:03,518 --> 00:00:06,944
I'm a principal engineer at
Target, and my work is AI

3
00:00:06,976 --> 00:00:09,293
infrastructure in agentic systems.

4
00:00:09,837 --> 00:00:14,661
Basically, the plumbing that lets, agents
using large language models call tools

5
00:00:14,675 --> 00:00:16,472
and act inside distributed systems.

6
00:00:17,470 --> 00:00:20,903
today we are going to talk about
scaling microservices observability

7
00:00:20,906 --> 00:00:23,954
with agentic, usage of language models.

8
00:00:24,674 --> 00:00:27,124
I want to be upfront about my angle.

9
00:00:27,350 --> 00:00:32,125
Most of the talks on this subject
are mostly focused on how to make

10
00:00:32,187 --> 00:00:33,944
an agent, a good investigator.

11
00:00:34,588 --> 00:00:36,896
how does that investigative agent reads?

12
00:00:37,146 --> 00:00:38,310
How does it read logs?

13
00:00:38,311 --> 00:00:43,544
How does it correlate traces and, and
propose candidates for root cause?

14
00:00:44,025 --> 00:00:46,183
I think that part is largely understood.

15
00:00:46,231 --> 00:00:49,225
The part that isn't
understood is the authority.

16
00:00:50,058 --> 00:00:52,753
What is the agent allowed to do?

17
00:00:53,319 --> 00:00:56,301
Who decides, what is it allowed to do?

18
00:00:56,331 --> 00:01:01,199
And how should that decision
change as an investigation goes on?

19
00:01:01,855 --> 00:01:04,413
that will be the focus of the talk.

20
00:01:04,557 --> 00:01:10,368
so basically what I am proposing
is diagnosis is the easy bit.

21
00:01:10,862 --> 00:01:13,709
Authority, is the hard part.

22
00:01:14,689 --> 00:01:20,277
So, So think about what an incident agent
actually does to-- So to be useful, an,

23
00:01:20,286 --> 00:01:26,519
an incident agent or investigative agent,
needs to access, all of your logs, your

24
00:01:26,519 --> 00:01:31,395
traces, your metrics, deployment history,
and at some point of time, it might

25
00:01:31,395 --> 00:01:37,922
even start, having controls to restart,
roll back, in your deployment pipeline.

26
00:01:38,503 --> 00:01:40,996
That is a privileged
identity in production.

27
00:01:41,330 --> 00:01:45,393
Now think about what it reads,
log lines, error messages,

28
00:01:45,426 --> 00:01:47,040
request headers, stack traces.

29
00:01:47,787 --> 00:01:54,263
A large share of that text originates
outside our trust boundary.

30
00:01:54,952 --> 00:02:02,353
So, anyone who can send, a request to
our service can put words in our logs.

31
00:02:02,461 --> 00:02:09,204
So we have to build a privileged
actor whose main input is

32
00:02:09,226 --> 00:02:11,097
partly written by strangers.

33
00:02:11,213 --> 00:02:13,296
So how, how would you build that?

34
00:02:13,361 --> 00:02:18,717
In security terms, that is, a
confused deputy waiting to happen.

35
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:24,635
So in, in operational terms, it's the
most interesting, agent we will deploy

36
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:29,547
because the questions you can postpone
for the chatbot, you cannot postpone here.

37
00:02:30,642 --> 00:02:31,342
moving on.

38
00:02:32,198 --> 00:02:34,534
So, I'll go through the
investigation slide quickly

39
00:02:34,537 --> 00:02:35,832
because I want to be fair to it.

40
00:02:35,875 --> 00:02:39,438
It matters, and it is where most
of the engineering effort has gone.

41
00:02:40,033 --> 00:02:44,802
Four habits separate a credible
investigative agent from a demo.

42
00:02:44,945 --> 00:02:46,370
First is grounding.

43
00:02:46,887 --> 00:02:51,226
So if the agent says the checkout
service caused the timeout, it

44
00:02:51,228 --> 00:02:57,406
cites, the trace ID, the timestamp,
and maybe some log statements,

45
00:02:57,406 --> 00:02:59,451
and then gives us links for those.

46
00:02:59,851 --> 00:03:05,115
So if it's unable to cite, obviously
it's hallucinating and, and there

47
00:03:05,116 --> 00:03:07,922
is no claim, there is no finding.

48
00:03:08,668 --> 00:03:12,321
Secondly, it, it tests
rather than, narrates.

49
00:03:12,372 --> 00:03:15,969
A, a language model will happily
tell you a plausible story.

50
00:03:16,983 --> 00:03:23,042
But, a real investigative agent needs
to turn that story into a prediction

51
00:03:23,266 --> 00:03:29,410
that a query can refute and, and runs
the refuting query first before it, it

52
00:03:29,411 --> 00:03:36,598
provides you the story so it needs to be
very methodical in its, in, in, in the

53
00:03:36,608 --> 00:03:40,103
theories, and analysis that it provides.

54
00:03:41,147 --> 00:03:44,201
third, step that it takes
is staged retrieval.

55
00:03:44,226 --> 00:03:49,658
So it starts with cheap aggregates,
then pulls a few traces, then, then

56
00:03:49,685 --> 00:03:52,647
it could maybe open a, a log window.

57
00:03:53,282 --> 00:03:57,785
a gateway enforces the budget so the
agent can't take down your entire

58
00:03:57,809 --> 00:04:00,140
telemetry while investigating an outage.

59
00:04:00,768 --> 00:04:03,418
And finally, it needs
to be honest about gaps.

60
00:04:03,591 --> 00:04:05,618
Sampling drops, spans.

61
00:04:05,658 --> 00:04:08,106
A missing edge is not evidence of health.

62
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:13,557
"Undersolved, but here is the next probe"
is a good answer instead of hallucinating.

63
00:04:13,562 --> 00:04:18,887
So, so one of the main, aspects of
investigative agent is to ground

64
00:04:18,889 --> 00:04:25,038
itself and, and to be able to tell
what, is covered and what isn't.

65
00:04:25,809 --> 00:04:30,091
So if it does all four of these, then
we have a pretty good investigator.

66
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:34,944
And, and notice that if it gets
any of these wrong, it leads

67
00:04:34,944 --> 00:04:39,641
to an engineer losing time and
which is, not an ideal case.

68
00:04:40,779 --> 00:04:44,148
So here is the asymmetry that
the rest of this talk is about.

69
00:04:44,903 --> 00:04:49,225
On the investigation side, the agent
touches logs, metrics, and traces.

70
00:04:49,740 --> 00:04:51,268
When it is wrong, an agent…

71
00:04:51,308 --> 00:04:54,483
an engineer loses time, and
a person reading the summary

72
00:04:54,485 --> 00:04:56,726
usu-usually catches the mistake.

73
00:04:57,805 --> 00:05:01,068
We protect that side with
read-only scopes and query

74
00:05:01,068 --> 00:05:03,435
budgets, and those generally work.

75
00:05:03,862 --> 00:05:07,377
But on the action side, the
agent can touch running systems.

76
00:05:08,202 --> 00:05:12,565
When it is wrong, customers can
lose service, and nobody is reading

77
00:05:12,567 --> 00:05:16,222
along in the same way, so the
error often shows up as an impact.

78
00:05:16,758 --> 00:05:18,509
Look at the bottom right cell.

79
00:05:19,212 --> 00:05:24,320
In many published designs, the safeguard
on the action side is a single approve

80
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:26,544
or deny prompt in front of a human.

81
00:05:27,154 --> 00:05:33,611
Every action gets the same prompt, whether
it is restarting one pod or changing

82
00:05:33,611 --> 00:05:35,449
network policy for a whole region.

83
00:05:36,638 --> 00:05:39,981
And after the 20th prompt of the
night, the on-call engineer is

84
00:05:39,982 --> 00:05:41,859
clicking approve, approve, approve.

85
00:05:42,075 --> 00:05:45,864
So separating read from write
is the right first move, but it

86
00:05:45,867 --> 00:05:47,894
only tells you where the line is.

87
00:05:48,436 --> 00:05:52,938
It doesn't tell you how much scrutiny
a given action deserves, and it tells

88
00:05:52,938 --> 00:05:59,373
you nothing about the path that the
agent took to get there Let me make the

89
00:05:59,374 --> 00:06:04,425
input problem concrete with two pieces
of published research from this year.

90
00:06:04,953 --> 00:06:09,058
The first is a benchmark called LogJack.

91
00:06:09,586 --> 00:06:14,803
The authors, planted prompt injection
payloads in cloud logs and gave those

92
00:06:14,824 --> 00:06:19,064
logs to debugging agents built on
eight different foundation models.

93
00:06:19,595 --> 00:06:23,481
On six of the eight, the agent
executed a remote command that

94
00:06:23,483 --> 00:06:24,937
came from the log content.

95
00:06:25,472 --> 00:06:31,612
The attacker needed no cloud permissions
at all, only the ability to influence

96
00:06:31,803 --> 00:06:33,899
an error message in the logs.

97
00:06:34,398 --> 00:06:38,239
The second is a NeurIPS paper
on security operations agents.

98
00:06:38,835 --> 00:06:44,516
Across more than 1,100 payloads,
e-e-every agent configuration

99
00:06:44,526 --> 00:06:49,043
they tested could be talked into
downgrading real malicious activity.

100
00:06:49,493 --> 00:06:54,482
So there was, some sort of
influence to even downplay things.

101
00:06:54,904 --> 00:06:59,236
And the detail I find most important,
when they let the agent pull additional

102
00:06:59,253 --> 00:07:04,344
read-only telemetry to cross-check,
that did not reliably fix it.

103
00:07:04,764 --> 00:07:08,093
That second finding does matter to us.

104
00:07:08,616 --> 00:07:12,025
The standard advice is verify
with independent telemetry.

105
00:07:12,616 --> 00:07:14,306
That helps with honest mistakes.

106
00:07:14,661 --> 00:07:19,355
It is not a defense against an adversary
who's writing into your telemetry.

107
00:07:19,562 --> 00:07:26,787
So treat every log, statement as an
unvalidated user input, and accept that

108
00:07:26,803 --> 00:07:33,371
moral reading-- accept that the moral
reading it will sometimes be fooled.

109
00:07:33,916 --> 00:07:37,714
The design question is
what we need to look into.

110
00:07:38,589 --> 00:07:42,006
so the second problem is harder to
see, and it doesn't need an attacker.

111
00:07:42,019 --> 00:07:45,495
That is the illustrative
investigation, not a real incident.

112
00:07:46,022 --> 00:07:47,854
The agent reads error logs.

113
00:07:48,563 --> 00:07:49,602
Yeah, that's reasonable.

114
00:07:49,648 --> 00:07:50,704
It queries traces.

115
00:07:50,916 --> 00:07:51,768
That's fine too.

116
00:07:52,188 --> 00:07:53,925
Treats the service configuration.

117
00:07:53,939 --> 00:07:55,044
That is still okay.

118
00:07:55,584 --> 00:07:57,548
Then it starts to act.

119
00:07:57,562 --> 00:08:00,615
It restarts one pod, which
is a standard first move.

120
00:08:01,432 --> 00:08:05,096
The systems persist, so it
restarts the whole service.

121
00:08:05,139 --> 00:08:09,420
System still persists, so it concludes
the problem is connectivity and

122
00:08:09,420 --> 00:08:11,751
proposes a network policy change.

123
00:08:12,175 --> 00:08:17,610
If you put an approval prompt in front
of each of those steps and judged

124
00:08:17,669 --> 00:08:21,966
each one alone, each one would pass.

125
00:08:22,348 --> 00:08:25,019
Restarting a pod during
an incident is normal.

126
00:08:25,228 --> 00:08:28,577
Escalating, when that
doesn't work is also normal.

127
00:08:29,224 --> 00:08:35,485
But as you look at this whole pattern
on the whole, the agent started as

128
00:08:36,730 --> 00:08:42,007
Basically a scraping, and in, in
the role of a scraping reader, and

129
00:08:42,007 --> 00:08:44,335
it ended up editing network policy.

130
00:08:44,679 --> 00:08:47,155
At no point did anything raise the bar.

131
00:08:47,362 --> 00:08:52,397
The height of these bars is the
thing nobody was measuring, the

132
00:08:52,399 --> 00:08:54,609
accumulated risk of the path.

133
00:08:55,059 --> 00:08:57,492
Engineers know this pattern
from human incidents.

134
00:08:57,568 --> 00:09:01,879
The dangerous moment is the fourth
mitigation that didn't work when

135
00:09:01,881 --> 00:09:07,759
some- someone tried, someone
tired reaches for a bigger lever.

136
00:09:08,328 --> 00:09:10,068
We have norms for that with people.

137
00:09:10,098 --> 00:09:13,094
We need a mechanism for it with agents.

138
00:09:14,258 --> 00:09:18,687
So if the last slide, slide
felt a little hypothetical, the

139
00:09:18,693 --> 00:09:21,319
cascading of events, it is not.

140
00:09:21,684 --> 00:09:26,237
In July of this year, research agents
running an internal cybersecurity eval

141
00:09:26,237 --> 00:09:30,836
at OpenAI got caught out of their sandbox
and into Hugging Face's production.

142
00:09:30,841 --> 00:09:33,665
This was a ve- very famous incident.

143
00:09:34,274 --> 00:09:38,506
Both companies have published, detailed
accounts, both OpenAI and Hugging Face,

144
00:09:38,507 --> 00:09:40,259
and I'd encourage you to read them.

145
00:09:40,851 --> 00:09:44,303
there are two numbers which,
which were interesting.

146
00:09:44,313 --> 00:09:48,094
Hugging Face's forensic
reconstruction covers roughly

147
00:09:48,109 --> 00:09:53,113
17,600 actions over four days.

148
00:09:53,144 --> 00:09:56,437
And according to OpenAI's report,
the agents went from a single

149
00:09:56,457 --> 00:10:02,811
dataset pod to cluster admin across
multiple clusters in under 13 hours.

150
00:10:03,671 --> 00:10:04,951
I want to be careful here.

151
00:10:05,007 --> 00:10:07,653
These are not incident response agents.

152
00:10:07,666 --> 00:10:11,019
The-- and this was an
evaluation environment and

153
00:10:11,019 --> 00:10:12,698
not operations team tooling.

154
00:10:13,752 --> 00:10:20,440
So I'm not claiming that typical
observability agents will do this, but

155
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:23,004
the lesson to take is a little narrower.

156
00:10:23,054 --> 00:10:26,450
It is, it is more on
the architectural side.

157
00:10:26,942 --> 00:10:31,986
There was no single action you could
point to and say that one was the breach.

158
00:10:32,013 --> 00:10:34,364
It is more like the trajectory.

159
00:10:34,889 --> 00:10:37,293
Thousands of individually small steps.

160
00:10:37,748 --> 00:10:42,928
Any control that evaluates one call at a
time with no memory of what came before

161
00:10:42,962 --> 00:10:45,074
is structurally unable to see that.

162
00:10:45,578 --> 00:10:48,919
So if we are going to give
agents authority in production,

163
00:10:49,294 --> 00:10:52,051
the control has to have memory.

164
00:10:52,056 --> 00:10:57,138
It needs to be able to see things
holistically So, this is where

165
00:10:57,176 --> 00:11:01,588
we kind of go back to the, to the
title of the talk, 'cause I think

166
00:11:01,595 --> 00:11:04,620
observability has a second job now.

167
00:11:05,370 --> 00:11:09,662
We've spent almost a decade
learning to instrument services,

168
00:11:09,662 --> 00:11:13,382
traces, metrics, logs, a shared
schema through OpenTelemetry.

169
00:11:13,949 --> 00:11:19,057
The incident agent consumes all of that,
but the agent is itself a new service

170
00:11:19,064 --> 00:11:24,226
in our system with far more freedom
than any microservice that we run.

171
00:11:24,673 --> 00:11:29,027
So it needs to be the best
instrumented thing that we own.

172
00:11:30,035 --> 00:11:34,952
Concretely, every tool call that
the agent makes should emit a

173
00:11:35,017 --> 00:11:37,694
typed event before it executes.

174
00:11:38,227 --> 00:11:43,195
Not a log line of free text, a record
with fields, a system of record,

175
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:46,985
that's, that's showing change that
the agent is proposing to make.

176
00:11:47,049 --> 00:11:48,791
What is the effect of this action?

177
00:11:50,074 --> 00:11:50,777
is it none?

178
00:11:50,896 --> 00:11:53,231
Is it reversible or,
or is it irreversible?

179
00:11:53,264 --> 00:11:54,769
What is the scope of the action?

180
00:11:54,769 --> 00:11:56,960
Is it one pod, a service, a region?

181
00:11:57,674 --> 00:11:58,917
What is its provenance?

182
00:11:59,009 --> 00:12:02,757
Meaning which inputs influenced
this call, and did any of them

183
00:12:02,758 --> 00:12:04,809
come from untrusted content?

184
00:12:05,501 --> 00:12:07,164
And then the governance fields.

185
00:12:07,236 --> 00:12:11,689
What level of scrutiny was required
and what was granted, and by whom?

186
00:12:12,122 --> 00:12:14,383
On the right is the illustrative record.

187
00:12:15,101 --> 00:12:18,706
The values are made up, but the
shape, is the one that matters.

188
00:12:19,102 --> 00:12:21,655
And one rule makes this enforceable.

189
00:12:21,712 --> 00:12:26,994
If a tool has no declared
metadata, the call fails closed.

190
00:12:27,729 --> 00:12:31,985
An action the system can't
classify does not run.

191
00:12:33,489 --> 00:12:37,310
Once actions are typed, you can
do something simple and powerful.

192
00:12:37,473 --> 00:12:39,420
Let the action itself set a floor.

193
00:12:40,598 --> 00:12:43,412
So you can read this
table from, top to bottom.

194
00:12:43,414 --> 00:12:47,659
Querying telemetry has no effect on
the system, so it sits at tier zero.

195
00:12:47,763 --> 00:12:48,948
Hopefully, right?

196
00:12:49,502 --> 00:12:52,092
The, the agent just does it and we record.

197
00:12:52,612 --> 00:12:56,934
Opening an incident and posting
a summary is-- it is reversible,

198
00:12:57,194 --> 00:12:58,617
and it affects maybe one team.

199
00:12:59,230 --> 00:13:00,473
Still, it is tier zero.

200
00:13:01,018 --> 00:13:04,057
Restarting a pod is also reversible.

201
00:13:04,776 --> 00:13:07,109
Touches one instance, so
it gets a light check.

202
00:13:07,592 --> 00:13:12,753
Scaling a re-- and/or restarting a whole
service touches more, so the floor rises.

203
00:13:13,225 --> 00:13:17,505
Changing network policy or rolling back
deployment is hard to undo and touches

204
00:13:17,555 --> 00:13:19,699
many services, so that needs a person.

205
00:13:20,695 --> 00:13:24,690
And at the bottom, anything
irreversible or anything that crosses

206
00:13:24,691 --> 00:13:29,176
a tenant boundary never goes below
human approval, no matter what.

207
00:13:29,794 --> 00:13:31,107
Two things about this table.

208
00:13:31,132 --> 00:13:36,592
First, the, the specific assignments
here are illustrative defaults.

209
00:13:37,176 --> 00:13:40,609
Where exactly you put a restart
depends on your system, and

210
00:13:40,656 --> 00:13:42,777
every operator will tune that.

211
00:13:43,242 --> 00:13:47,438
Second, and more important, the
floor depends on the action and never

212
00:13:47,438 --> 00:13:49,557
on how confident the agent sounds.

213
00:13:50,193 --> 00:13:53,975
An agent that is very sure about
deleting data still needs a human.

214
00:13:54,142 --> 00:13:56,112
Confidence is not a credential.

215
00:13:56,967 --> 00:13:58,966
I'll explain the tiers in the next slide.

216
00:13:59,950 --> 00:14:03,751
So here is the approach that we
are proposing, that I'm working

217
00:14:03,752 --> 00:14:05,383
on as an open reference design.

218
00:14:05,383 --> 00:14:07,441
I call it guarded oversight.

219
00:14:08,198 --> 00:14:14,041
Today, most systems have two settings:
the agent acts alone or a human approves.

220
00:14:14,838 --> 00:14:17,187
That is still a bit too coarse.

221
00:14:17,228 --> 00:14:22,583
It either leaves risk unexamined
or it buries the on-call engineer.

222
00:14:23,366 --> 00:14:26,632
So we need to put some steps in between.

223
00:14:26,778 --> 00:14:28,512
Tier zero is observe.

224
00:14:28,549 --> 00:14:29,403
Observe only.

225
00:14:29,934 --> 00:14:31,720
The action runs and is recorded.

226
00:14:31,885 --> 00:14:33,335
Tier one is justify.

227
00:14:33,356 --> 00:14:37,695
The agent must state why it wants
to take this action in a fixed

228
00:14:37,695 --> 00:14:42,720
structure, and a deterministic rule
that check-checks that justification

229
00:14:42,755 --> 00:14:44,292
against the evidence it cites.

230
00:14:44,435 --> 00:14:45,274
This is cheap.

231
00:14:46,083 --> 00:14:50,393
And the idea is that an agent
that has wandered off task will

232
00:14:50,403 --> 00:14:52,029
struggle to fill in the form.

233
00:14:52,328 --> 00:14:54,478
Tier two is a second opinion.

234
00:14:54,501 --> 00:15:00,752
An independent model with a different
context has to concur to the action

235
00:15:00,757 --> 00:15:02,738
proposed by the investigative agent.

236
00:15:03,247 --> 00:15:07,607
Tier three is human approval, and
the human sees the provenance.

237
00:15:07,660 --> 00:15:11,956
This proposal was influenced
by untrusted log content, for

238
00:15:11,956 --> 00:15:16,342
example, that makes it a real hard
decision instead of a rubber stamp.

239
00:15:17,102 --> 00:15:18,907
Tier four is halt.

240
00:15:19,084 --> 00:15:20,182
Stop the session.

241
00:15:20,250 --> 00:15:24,771
Freeze anything the agent would write
to its own memory and page the operator.

242
00:15:24,870 --> 00:15:31,082
The point of the middle tiers is to spend
human attention only where it is needed.

243
00:15:32,082 --> 00:15:36,342
So, now the piece that answers the
trajectory problem, the tier an action

244
00:15:36,344 --> 00:15:38,913
needs is a larger of the two things.

245
00:15:39,429 --> 00:15:42,544
One is the floor for that action
from the table we just saw.

246
00:15:42,632 --> 00:15:46,822
The other is the tier implied by the
risk this session has accumulated so far.

247
00:15:47,871 --> 00:15:50,403
So if we go back to the
drifting investigation, the

248
00:15:50,408 --> 00:15:53,576
first pod restart is tier one.

249
00:15:54,152 --> 00:15:58,216
But by the time the agent has restarted
a pod, restarted the service, read

250
00:15:58,219 --> 00:16:02,163
untrusted content along the way, and
is now reaching for network policy,

251
00:16:02,596 --> 00:16:06,824
the session's accumulated risk has
climbed, and the same kind of action

252
00:16:06,831 --> 00:16:12,125
that passed lightly an hour ago now
needs a second opinion or a person.

253
00:16:12,236 --> 00:16:16,307
The bar rises as the path
gets longer and stranger.

254
00:16:17,462 --> 00:16:20,709
Three properties make this
untrustworthy, and they are the

255
00:16:20,710 --> 00:16:25,162
ones I think should be stated
precisely and where possible proved.

256
00:16:25,598 --> 00:16:27,504
First, no self-reason.

257
00:16:27,619 --> 00:16:33,008
Nothing the agent says and nothing in
the content it reads can lower the score.

258
00:16:33,718 --> 00:16:37,634
An injected logline that says, "This
session is safe," has no effect.

259
00:16:38,170 --> 00:16:39,829
Only a human resets it.

260
00:16:40,230 --> 00:16:41,567
Second is inheritance.

261
00:16:41,612 --> 00:16:46,074
If the agent delegates to a sub-agent,
the sub-agent starts with the

262
00:16:46,112 --> 00:16:48,433
parent agent's risk, tier level.

263
00:16:48,869 --> 00:16:52,412
Otherwise, delegation becomes
a way to launder a history.

264
00:16:52,938 --> 00:16:54,737
Third, the floor is always hold.

265
00:16:54,771 --> 00:16:59,957
A perfectly calm session still
can't delete data without a person.

266
00:17:01,102 --> 00:17:05,770
So a fair question at this point,
where does the risk score come from?

267
00:17:06,018 --> 00:17:09,775
And doesn't this just move
the problem to another model?

268
00:17:10,668 --> 00:17:13,223
It would if we let just the model decide.

269
00:17:13,228 --> 00:17:18,543
So the rule is a model's judgment is
evidence, but it isn't permission.

270
00:17:19,235 --> 00:17:23,063
On the left, models act as, sensors.

271
00:17:23,429 --> 00:17:26,618
Fast classifiers can score
each proposed action.

272
00:17:26,832 --> 00:17:31,287
Does the content that influenced it
look like it contains instructions?

273
00:17:32,148 --> 00:17:35,198
Is this action a departure
from the stated task?

274
00:17:35,342 --> 00:17:37,198
How large is the blast radius?

275
00:17:37,236 --> 00:17:38,867
Those are the useful signals.

276
00:17:39,237 --> 00:17:43,837
But each one comes with an error rate,
and you should measure that error rate

277
00:17:43,840 --> 00:17:46,221
in a shadow mode before you rely on it.

278
00:17:46,672 --> 00:17:49,535
And you combine the signals
conservatively, assuming

279
00:17:49,535 --> 00:17:51,109
that they can fail together.

280
00:17:51,694 --> 00:17:54,328
On the right, authority lives in code.

281
00:17:54,638 --> 00:17:58,161
Deterministic policy
takes the typed action.

282
00:17:58,703 --> 00:18:02,597
Its provenance and sensor
readings decides the tier.

283
00:18:03,885 --> 00:18:06,069
That component is meant to be small.

284
00:18:06,101 --> 00:18:07,189
It's a state machine.

285
00:18:07,299 --> 00:18:11,593
Small enough to test exhaustively, small
enough that you can prove properties

286
00:18:11,598 --> 00:18:16,121
about it, like the no self-reset
property from the previous slide.

287
00:18:16,568 --> 00:18:18,675
That division is the heart of the design.

288
00:18:19,232 --> 00:18:26,084
That-- the, the parts that can be fooled
sit at the edge and treated as fallible.

289
00:18:26,630 --> 00:18:31,837
The part that grants authority can't
be argued with, and if untrusted input

290
00:18:31,849 --> 00:18:38,965
tries to talk its own score down, that
attempt itself is a policy violation.

291
00:18:40,383 --> 00:18:43,521
So how do we know if any of this can work?

292
00:18:44,320 --> 00:18:48,936
So I want to offer four measurements, and
I ask them of any vendor or internal team

293
00:18:48,936 --> 00:18:51,644
that tells you their agent is safe to ask.

294
00:18:51,655 --> 00:18:53,869
First, catch tier distribution.

295
00:18:54,625 --> 00:18:59,839
Take a set of unsafe actions
from benchmarks and from your own

296
00:18:59,854 --> 00:19:03,594
replayed incidents, and record
which tiers stopped each one.

297
00:19:04,128 --> 00:19:09,580
If everything is caught at tier three
by the human, your lower tiers aren't

298
00:19:09,587 --> 00:19:12,955
doing anything, and people will burn out.

299
00:19:13,504 --> 00:19:17,866
If things are caught at tier one and
two, the design is earning its keep.

300
00:19:18,448 --> 00:19:20,969
Secondly, approvals per shift.

301
00:19:21,009 --> 00:19:26,802
Count the human decisions the gate demands
from one on-call engineer in one shift.

302
00:19:27,303 --> 00:19:30,305
This is the approval fatigue
number, and it is a budget.

303
00:19:30,323 --> 00:19:31,401
You have to budget it.

304
00:19:31,844 --> 00:19:35,794
If you can ex-- If you exceed it,
people stop reading and just start

305
00:19:35,795 --> 00:19:38,095
clicking yes, yes, yes, approve.

306
00:19:38,955 --> 00:19:40,923
Third is the utility cost.

307
00:19:40,957 --> 00:19:45,416
With the gate on, how many legitimate
investigations still complete?

308
00:19:45,751 --> 00:19:50,040
A gate that blocks everything is
perfectly safe, but it's useless.

309
00:19:50,761 --> 00:19:54,022
The-there are no investigations
that complete and, and

310
00:19:54,023 --> 00:19:55,343
are providing resolution.

311
00:19:56,362 --> 00:19:59,544
And finally, there is replay miss rate.

312
00:20:00,111 --> 00:20:04,831
Run the whole thing in shadow
against your past incidents and

313
00:20:04,833 --> 00:20:07,262
count what it would have let through.

314
00:20:07,621 --> 00:20:09,430
I'm not showing you
results for these today.

315
00:20:09,820 --> 00:20:12,494
The reference implementation
and evaluation harness are in

316
00:20:12,494 --> 00:20:16,335
progress, and I'd rather show you
numbers when I have real ones.

317
00:20:16,706 --> 00:20:20,679
What I'm arguing is that these are
the right four numbers to look at

318
00:20:21,679 --> 00:20:23,800
So if you want to start on
Monday, here is the order.

319
00:20:23,818 --> 00:20:25,309
Stage one, replay.

320
00:20:25,463 --> 00:20:28,308
Take your past instance with
their telemetry and run the

321
00:20:28,309 --> 00:20:29,672
agent against them offline.

322
00:20:29,847 --> 00:20:31,042
No live access at all.

323
00:20:31,646 --> 00:20:34,219
You learn how good the
investigator is, and you start

324
00:20:34,219 --> 00:20:35,622
collecting the phone numbers.

325
00:20:36,082 --> 00:20:38,328
Stage two is read-only in production.

326
00:20:38,461 --> 00:20:41,816
The agent investigates real live
incidents and writes summaries.

327
00:20:41,898 --> 00:20:42,970
It can't change anything.

328
00:20:43,779 --> 00:20:45,554
Meanwhile, the gate runs in shadow.

329
00:20:45,598 --> 00:20:49,719
It records what tier it
would have required for the,

330
00:20:49,973 --> 00:20:51,512
actions the agent proposed.

331
00:20:51,657 --> 00:20:55,341
You compare that to what
your engineers actually did.

332
00:20:56,188 --> 00:20:58,083
Stage three is reversible actions.

333
00:20:58,114 --> 00:21:01,493
Now the agent can restart and
scale through the tires with every

334
00:21:01,497 --> 00:21:03,262
decision recorded as telemetry.

335
00:21:04,066 --> 00:21:06,740
And then finally, stage
four is wider authority.

336
00:21:07,282 --> 00:21:11,886
And, and the rule for this stage
is that you expand only, as far

337
00:21:11,888 --> 00:21:13,755
as your catch tier data supports.

338
00:21:14,206 --> 00:21:18,511
If the data says the middle tiers
are catching drift reliably, extend.

339
00:21:18,831 --> 00:21:20,340
If not, do not.

340
00:21:20,856 --> 00:21:24,103
What I like about this order
is that each stage produces the

341
00:21:24,103 --> 00:21:26,205
evidence that justifies the next.

342
00:21:26,337 --> 00:21:32,292
You never have to take the agent's safety
on faith, and you never have to take mine.

343
00:21:32,816 --> 00:21:36,647
So, I just want to close out,
basically where, we started.

344
00:21:36,659 --> 00:21:42,573
The title of this talk is about scaling
observability with agentic LLMs.

345
00:21:42,627 --> 00:21:44,215
we've argued about two things.

346
00:21:45,198 --> 00:21:49,092
What it means for agents that read your
telemetry and help you investigate.

347
00:21:49,504 --> 00:21:52,366
That's the easy half, and
that's the real value.

348
00:21:53,004 --> 00:21:57,394
It means telemetry about your agents,
typed, recorded, and used to decide how

349
00:21:57,396 --> 00:21:59,662
much authority they get at each step.

350
00:21:59,667 --> 00:22:00,855
That is the hard half.

351
00:22:01,457 --> 00:22:03,678
If you take one sentence
away, take this one.

352
00:22:03,685 --> 00:22:06,808
Scale the oversight with the agent.

353
00:22:07,458 --> 00:22:12,552
As you give it more reach, the scrutiny
has to grow with it automatically in a

354
00:22:12,552 --> 00:22:15,848
way the agent can't talk its way out of.

355
00:22:16,429 --> 00:22:17,712
Two notes on what's next.

356
00:22:18,196 --> 00:22:22,045
I am working on open reference design
and an evaluation harness for the

357
00:22:22,048 --> 00:22:24,498
graduated oversight approach I described.

358
00:22:24,880 --> 00:22:28,228
I'll be publishing them when
they're ready to be scrutinized.

359
00:22:29,065 --> 00:22:31,305
And this isn't only an
engineering concern.

360
00:22:31,336 --> 00:22:36,148
In February, NIST's, s-center of AI
Standards and Innovation, they la-launched

361
00:22:36,154 --> 00:22:40,605
an AI agent standard initiative and
asked for input on agent scrutiny.

362
00:22:41,015 --> 00:22:45,184
I have a comment submitted there
with some perspective, of somebody

363
00:22:45,184 --> 00:22:47,118
who runs and deploys these systems.

364
00:22:47,191 --> 00:22:51,945
So if you run agents in production, your
experience is exactly what that process

365
00:22:51,946 --> 00:22:54,618
needs, and I encourage you to take part.

366
00:22:55,218 --> 00:22:56,437
Thank you for your time.

